
#figure(
  kind: table,
  caption: [
    Known results for (L4-)liveness and the liveness levels, and their implementation status.
    Direction: $⇔$ the condition characterises the property, $⇒$ the condition is sufficient, $⇐$ the condition is necessary.
  ],
  text(size: 6.5pt, table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: left + top,
    inset: 3pt,
    stroke: 0.4pt + luma(170),
    table.header([*Applicability*], [*Condition*], [*Direction*], [*Theoretical complexity*], [*Citation*], [*Implementation status*], [*Description*]),
    [Circuit],
    [$M_0(S) > 0$],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|P|)$],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.1.3]; @murata_petri_1989[Thm. 4, Thm. 7]],
    [Implemented],
    [A circuit is both an S-net and a T-net; the two liveness theorems coincide.],

    [State machine],
    [Strongly connected and $M_0(S) > 0$],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|P| + |T|)$],
    [@murata_petri_1989[Thm. 4]; @esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.1.3]; @best_petri_2024[Cor. 5.30]],
    [Implemented],
    [The Primer states it as: covered by cycles and every strongly connected component marked. Equivalent here, because petrivet only admits connected nets.],

    [State machine],
    [On the SCC condensation: transitions between SCCs are L1; transitions inside a reachable nontrivial SCC are L3; L4 if that SCC is the only terminal nontrivial SCC reachable from some marked SCC; L0 if the SCC is unmarked and unreachable],
    [$⇔$ (per transition)],
    [$O(m (|P| + |T|))$, $m$ = number of marked SCCs],
    [Own derivation (builds on @best_petri_2024[Cor. 5.30])],
    [Implemented],
    [Not found in the sources, so it needs a proof sketch in the thesis. Tests: `s_net_non_sc_mixed_levels`, `s_net_unreachable_sink_l0`.],

    [Marked graph],
    [Every circuit carries at least one token at $M_0$],
    [$⇔$],
    [P (cycle search in the subgraph of unmarked places)],
    [@murata_petri_1989[Thm. 7]; @esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.2.3]; @best_petri_2024[Thm. 5.31]],
    [Implemented],
    [Circuit token counts are invariant, so an unmarked circuit stays dead forever.],

    [Marked graph],
    [Transitions on an unmarked circuit are L0; transitions reachable from them over unmarked places only are L0, over a path containing a marked place L1; all other transitions are L4],
    [$⇔$ (per transition)],
    [P],
    [Own derivation (builds on @murata_petri_1989[Thm. 6, Thm. 7])],
    [Implemented],
    [Needs a proof sketch. Test: `t_net_dead_predecessor_propagates`.],

    [Free-choice],
    [Commoner–Hack: every proper siphon contains a trap that is marked at $M_0$],
    [$⇔$],
    [co-NP-complete (non-liveness is NP-complete); exponentially many minimal siphons],
    [@murata_petri_1989[Thm. 12]; @best_petri_2024[Thm. 5.17, Prop. 5.28]; @esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.3.4, Thm. 5.3.11]; @hack_analysis_1972; @jones_complexity_1977],
    [Implemented],
    [Implemented as an SMT-based siphon-trap search @oanea_new_2010. Not polynomial, so "efficient" in the API is a misnomer for this row. Finding the maximal trap inside a given siphon is polynomial.],

    [Free-choice],
    [Rank theorem: positive S- and T-invariants exist, $"rank"(C) = c - 1$ with $c$ the number of clusters, and every proper siphon is marked at $M_0$],
    [$⇔$ ("live and bounded")],
    [P; $O(|P| * |T| * |F|)$ for the structural part],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.3.16]; @best_petri_2024[Lem. 6.17, Thm. 6.18]; @esparza_analysis_1991[Cor. 2.8]; @kemper_efficient_1992],
    [Not implemented],
    [Decides liveness and boundedness jointly in polynomial time, although liveness alone is co-NP-complete. Worthwhile whenever boundedness is wanted as well.],

    [Free-choice],
    [Sound and complete reduction rules preserve liveness and boundedness; decide the reduced net],
    [$⇔$ (reduction)],
    [P],
    [@esparza_analysis_1991[Thm. 3.9]],
    [Not implemented],
    [The reduction kits are complete for live and bounded free-choice systems.],

    [Asymmetric-choice],
    [Every proper siphon contains a trap that is marked at $M_0$],
    [$⇒$],
    [Refutation is NP-complete],
    [@murata_petri_1989[Thm. 15]; @oanea_new_2010],
    [Implemented],
    [Not necessary: Murata's Fig. 35 is a live AC net with a siphon that contains no marked trap. Failure of the criterion is inconclusive and yields `None`.],

    [Asymmetric-choice],
    [Live iff place-live: from every reachable marking, every place can eventually be marked],
    [$⇔$],
    [No cheaper than the state-space based procedures],
    [@murata_petri_1989[§V-B]],
    [Not implemented],
    [Stated in Murata without proof. A source containing the proof still needs to be located.],

    [General],
    [Every proper siphon is marked at $M_0$],
    [$⇐$],
    [P (maximal siphon inside the set of unmarked places)],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Prop. 4.4.5]; @best_petri_2024[Alg. 6.19]],
    [Not implemented (`todo` in `is_efficiently_live`)],
    [Cheap refutation valid for every class: `Some(false)` as soon as an unmarked proper siphon exists.],

    [Bounded net (finite reachability graph)],
    [Every bottom SCC of the reachability graph contains an arc labelled by each transition (L4); the levels L0–L3 follow from SCC and cycle analysis],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|"RG"|)$, exponential in $|P|$; PSPACE-complete for 1-bounded nets],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Prop. 3.1.1]; @best_petri_2024[Prop. 3.11]; @murata_petri_1989[§V-C]],
    [Implemented],
    [The fallback. NB: for unbounded nets `liveness()` currently reports L0 for every transition, which conflates "unknown" with "dead".],

    [Unbounded net],
    [Open: reduce to reachability (@hack_decidability_1976) or analyse an extension of the coverability graph],
    [$⇔$],
    [Ackermann-hard, but decidable],
    [@best_petri_2024[§3.7]; @esparza_petri_2026[§3.2.5]],
    [Not implemented],
    [No complete procedure exists in petrivet; the API should report an explicit "unknown" instead of L0.],

    [General],
    [Structural reduction rules preserve liveness; analyse the smaller reduced net],
    [$⇔$ (equivalence-preserving)],
    [—],
    [@berthelot_reduction_nodate],
    [Not implemented],
    [See also the deadlock-freedom table: the reduction rules of Thierry-Mieg are stated for deadlocks and safety properties.],
  )),
) <tab:liveness-theorems>

#figure(
  kind: table,
  caption: [Known results for deadlock-freedom and their implementation status.
    Direction: $⇔$ the condition characterises the property, $⇒$ the condition is sufficient, $⇐$ the condition is necessary.],
  text(size: 6.5pt, table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: left + top,
    inset: 3pt,
    stroke: 0.4pt + luma(170),
    table.header([*Applicability*], [*Condition*], [*Direction*], [*Theoretical complexity*], [*Citation*], [*Implementation status*], [*Description*]),
    [General],
    [Some transition $t$ has an empty preset, $•t = ∅$],
    [$⇒$],
    [$O(|T|)$],
    [@thierry-mieg_structural_2020[Rule 6]],
    [Not implemented],
    [Such a transition is enabled in every marking. Matters most for unbounded nets, where the exploration fallback would never terminate.],

    [Live net],
    [Live implies deadlock-free: any liveness certificate also certifies deadlock-freedom],
    [$⇒$],
    [As the liveness procedure used],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Prop. 2.10.2 (1)]],
    [Implemented],
    [The converse fails (@best_petri_2024[Fig. 5.7]): deadlock-freedom is strictly weaker, so a negative liveness verdict says nothing about deadlock-freedom.],

    [Circuit],
    [$M_0(S) > 0$],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|P|)$],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.1.3, Prop. 2.10.2 (1)]],
    [Implemented],
    [If $M_0$ has no token, $M_0$ is itself a deadlock; otherwise the circuit is live.],

    [State machine],
    [$M_0(S) > 0$ and some transition is L4-live],
    [$⇒$],
    [$O(|P| + |T|)$],
    [Via liveness: @esparza_petri_2026[Prop. 2.10.2 (1)]],
    [Implemented (negative verdict unsound)],
    [NB: sufficient only, but the efficient check also returns `Some(false)` when the test fails. Counterexample: a single token that can reach two different terminal cycles is deadlock-free yet has no L4 transition.],

    [State machine],
    [$M_0 ≠ 0$ and some marked place cannot reach any place with an empty postset],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|P| + |T|)$ (backward search from the sink places)],
    [Own derivation (specialises @esparza_petri_2026[Prop. 4.4.6])],
    [Not implemented],
    [Tokens in a state machine move independently, so a deadlock is reachable iff every token can be steered onto a place with empty postset. Would replace the L4-based test by an exact one. Needs a proof sketch.],

    [Marked graph],
    [No unmarked circuit (the net is live)],
    [$⇒$],
    [P],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.2.3, Prop. 2.10.2 (1)]; @murata_petri_1989[Thm. 7]],
    [Implemented (negative verdict unsound)],
    [NB: an unmarked circuit next to an independent live circuit is deadlock-free, yet the efficient check returns `Some(false)`. Sound only as `Some(true)` unless the net is strongly connected (next row).],

    [Strongly connected marked graph],
    [No unmarked circuit; equivalently live, deadlock-free, or has an infinite firing sequence],
    [$⇔$],
    [P],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.2.8]],
    [Implemented (hypothesis not checked)],
    [Strong connectedness is needed so that the net is bounded and every transition occurs in any infinite run.],

    [Not strongly connected marked graph],
    [Open: combine the SCC condensation with the unmarked-circuit analysis],
    [$⇔$],
    [P],
    [Own derivation],
    [Not implemented],
    [Needed to make the marked-graph verdict total.],

    [General],
    [Commoner–Hack: every proper siphon contains a trap that is marked at $M_0$],
    [$⇒$],
    [Refutation is NP-complete; exponentially many minimal siphons],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Prop. 4.4.12 (via Prop. 4.4.6, Cor. 4.4.7, Prop. 4.4.10)]; @best_petri_2024[Cor. 5.18]; @oanea_new_2010],
    [Implemented],
    [Valid for every ordinary net: the unmarked places of a dead marking form a siphon, and a marked trap can never be emptied. Not polynomial, so not "efficient" in the strict sense.],

    [1-bounded net],
    [The system $M = M_0 + C X$, $sum_(s ∈ •t) M(s) < |•t|$ for all $t$ has no solution],
    [$⇒$],
    [NP (ILP)],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Prop. 4.2.9]],
    [Not implemented],
    [Semi-algorithm built on the marking equation. Could reuse the existing SMT infrastructure.],

    [Bounded net (finite reachability graph)],
    [The reachability graph has no vertex without successors],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|"RG"|)$; PSPACE-complete for 1-bounded nets],
    [@best_petri_2024[Prop. 3.11]; @esparza_petri_2026[§3.1.1]],
    [Implemented],
    [Breadth-first search behind the `deadlocks()` iterator. For unbounded nets only a semi-decision procedure: it terminates if a deadlock is reachable and may diverge otherwise.],

    [General],
    [Explore a stubborn-set reduced state space; it contains every deadlock of the full one],
    [$⇔$ (preserves deadlocks)],
    [Exponential worst case; often much smaller graphs],
    [@valmari_stubborn_1991],
    [Not implemented],
    [Orthogonal speed-up of the exploration fallback.],

    [General],
    [Reduce to finitely many reachability queries: for each place set $R$ hitting every preset, is a marking with $M(s) = 0$ for all $s ∈ R$ reachable?],
    [$⇔$],
    [Ackermann-hard (@czerwinski_reachability_2022; non-elementary already by @czerwinski_reachability_2020); exponentially many queries],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 3.2.25, Thm. 3.2.26, §3.2.5]; @best_petri_2024[§3.7]],
    [Not implemented],
    [A complete procedure for unbounded nets in principle. Could be driven by the existing CEGAR reachability engine.],

    [General],
    [Structural reduction rules that preserve deadlocks; analyse the smaller reduced net],
    [$⇔$ (equivalence-preserving)],
    [—],
    [@thierry-mieg_structural_2020[Sect. 5]; @berthelot_reduction_nodate],
    [Not implemented],
    [Thierry-Mieg's rules are stated explicitly for deadlock detection.],
  )),
) <tab:deadlock-theorems>

#figure(
  kind: table,
  caption: [Known results for reachability and their implementation status.
    Direction: $⇔$ the condition characterises the property, $⇒$ the condition is sufficient, $⇐$ the condition is necessary.],
  text(size: 6.5pt, table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: left + top,
    inset: 3pt,
    stroke: 0.4pt + luma(170),
    table.header([*Applicability*], [*Condition*], [*Direction*], [*Theoretical complexity*], [*Citation*], [*Implementation status*], [*Description*]),

    [Circuit],
    [$M(S) = M_0(S)$],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|P|)$],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.1.5]],
    [Implemented],
    [A circuit with a token is a live S-system. Without tokens only $M_0$ itself is reachable, which the condition also covers.],

    [Strongly connected state machine],
    [$M(S) = M_0(S)$],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|P|)$],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.1.5]],
    [Implemented],
    [Tokens can be moved to any place. The same equivalence is the state equation specialised to S-invariants.],

    [Not strongly connected state machine],
    [Open: needs a token-flow argument over the SCC condensation],
    [$⇔$],
    [—],
    [Own derivation],
    [Not implemented],
    [Currently falls through to the general CEGAR procedure.],

    [Live marked graph],
    [$M$ is reachable iff the marking equation $M = M_0 + C X$ has a solution],
    [$⇔$],
    [P (rational linear algebra suffices, no ILP)],
    [@murata_petri_1989[Thm. 19]; @esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.2.7]],
    [Not implemented (`todo` in code)],
    [The proof of the source shows that integrality and non-negativity of $X$ can always be recovered by adding multiples of the T-invariant $(1, …, 1)$, so the check is not NP-complete as stated in the docs of `NetClass::MarkedGraph`.],

    [Marked graph],
    [The marking equation has a solution and the minimal non-negative solution fires no transition on a token-free circuit],
    [$⇔$],
    [P],
    [@murata_petri_1989[Thm. 20]],
    [Not implemented],
    [Extends the live case to non-live marked graphs.],

    [Live and bounded free-choice net],
    [There is $X ∈ ℕ^(|T|)$ with $M = M_0 + C X$ such that $(N_U, M_U)$ has no unmarked trap, where $U = {t ∣ X(t) > 0}$ is the support of $X$],
    [$⇔$],
    [NP-complete],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.3.17, Thm. 5.3.19]; @desel_free_1995],
    [Not implemented],
    [NB: the docs of `NetClass::FreeChoice` define $U$ with $X(t) = 0$, whereas the sources use the support $X(t) > 0$. Then the source guesses $U$ and $X$ and checks the maximal trap of $N_U$.],

    [Live, bounded and cyclic free-choice net],
    [$M_0 ∼ M$; cyclic iff $M_0$ marks every proper trap],
    [$⇔$],
    [P],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.3.20, Thm. 5.3.21, Cor. 5.3.22]],
    [Not implemented],
    [Polynomial special case of the previous row.],

    [General],
    [State equation: $M = M_0 + C X$ has a solution $X ≥ 0$; every trap marked at $M_0$ is marked at $M$; every siphon marked at $M$ is marked at $M_0$],
    [$⇐$],
    [LP / ILP; trap and siphon checks are polynomial],
    [@murata_petri_1989[Thm. 2]; @esparza_petri_2026[Prop. 4.2.8, Cor. 4.4.4, Cor. 4.4.11]],
    [Implemented (as CEGAR lemmas)],
    [Over-approximations: failure proves unreachability, success is inconclusive.],

    [General],
    [CEGAR over the state equation, refined with traps, P-invariants, transition-ordering and increment constraints; candidate Parikh vectors are witnessed by guided exploration],
    [$⇔$],
    [The problem is Ackermann-complete (@czerwinski_reachability_2022); no bound is claimed for the procedure, which is often fast on unreachable instances],
    [@wimmel_applying_2011; @clarke_counterexample-guided_2000],
    [Implemented],
    [Sound in both directions (UNSAT of an over-approximation, explicit witness). The thesis needs a termination argument.],

    [Bounded net (finite reachability graph)],
    [Explore the reachability graph (breadth-first or depth-first) until the target is found or exhausted],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|"RG"|)$; PSPACE-complete for 1-bounded nets],
    [@best_petri_2024[Prop. 3.11]; @esparza_petri_2026[§3.1.1]],
    [Implemented (explorer, not used by `is_reachable`)],
    [Complete for bounded nets only. Useful as an independent oracle for testing the shortcuts and the CEGAR engine.],

    [General],
    [Decision procedures of Mayr, Kosaraju, Lambert; Leroux's variant runs two semi-decision procedures in parallel: state-space exploration and a search for a separating semilinear inductive invariant],
    [$⇔$],
    [Ackermann-complete (@czerwinski_reachability_2022); EXPSPACE-hard (@lipton_reachability_1976)],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 3.2.24, §3.2.5]; @best_petri_2024[§3.7]; @mayr_algorithm_1981; @kosaraju_decidability_1982; @lambert_structure_1992; @leroux_vector_2012],
    [Not implemented],
    [Out of scope as an implementation; cited for the complexity landscape.],
  )),
) <tab:reachability-theorems>

#figure(
  kind: table,
  caption: [Known results for coverability and their implementation status.
    Direction: $⇔$ the condition characterises the property, $⇒$ the condition is sufficient, $⇐$ the condition is necessary.],
  text(size: 6.5pt, table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: left + top,
    inset: 3pt,
    stroke: 0.4pt + luma(170),
    table.header([*Applicability*], [*Condition*], [*Direction*], [*Theoretical complexity*], [*Citation*], [*Implementation status*], [*Description*]),
    [Circuit],
    [$M_0(S) ≥ M(S)$],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|P|)$],
    [Corollary of @esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.1.5]],
    [Implemented],
    [Any marking $M' ≥ M$ with $M'(S) = M_0(S)$ is reachable, because tokens can be moved to any place.],

    [Strongly connected state machine],
    [$M_0(S) ≥ M(S)$],
    [$⇔$],
    [$O(|P|)$],
    [Corollary of @esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 5.1.5]],
    [Implemented],
    [Same argument as for circuits.],

    [General],
    [The Karp–Miller coverability graph contains an $ω$-marking $M'' ≥ M$],
    [$⇔$],
    [EXPSPACE-complete (@rackoff_covering_1978 @lipton_reachability_1976)],
    [@karp_parallel_1969; @esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 3.2.5, Thm. 3.2.8]; @best_petri_2024[Prop. 3.27]],
    [Implemented (graph construction)],
    [`is_coverable` currently dispatches to CEGAR, not to the coverability graph. The graph is also the engine for boundedness.],

    [General],
    [CEGAR over the state equation with target "$M(s) ≥$ target" (same engine as reachability)],
    [$⇔$],
    [The problem is EXPSPACE-complete; no bound is claimed for the procedure],
    [@wimmel_applying_2011; @clarke_counterexample-guided_2000],
    [Implemented],
    [Shares all refinements with the reachability procedure.],

    [General],
    [Backward search over upward-closed sets of markings; any covering run can be shortened to length $(2n)^((k+1)!)$ (Rackoff)],
    [$⇔$],
    [EXPSPACE],
    [@esparza_petri_2026[Thm. 3.2.9, Thm. 3.2.17, Thm. 3.2.18]; @abdulla_sat-solving_2004; @rackoff_covering_1978],
    [Not implemented],
    [The SAT-based variant of Abdulla et al. is a candidate use of petrivet's solver backend.],

    [General],
    [IC3 / property-directed reachability for well-structured transition systems, optionally with place-merge abstraction],
    [$⇔$],
    [EXPSPACE; sound, complete and terminating for downward-finite WSTS],
    [@kloos_incremental_2013; @kang_abstraction-based_2021],
    [Not implemented],
    [Competitive with the state of the art in the experiments of the sources. Natural future work next to the CEGAR engine.],
  )),
) <tab:coverability-theorems>